SM(173,{"n":"James 5","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/James 5.mp3","d":2575.5,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Council of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.64,"Chapter 5, verse 1."],[24.56,"Oh, this is gonna offend some people, but hey, it's St."],[27.28,"James. What you gonna do?"],[29.84,"Come now, you rich, weep and howl for your miseries that are coming upon you."],[36.96,"Um I've said before that uh you you you get a lot of folks who want to say we take everything in the Bible literally, and you get to that place where Christ says uh go sell everything you have and give it to the poor and and become my disciple, and all of a sudden people are like, well, you know, well, uh there's all the reasons why that doesn't that doesn't apply to me."],[62.4,"Well, here we go again."],[64.4,"Um so this is not this is not class envy."],[74.56,"This is not this is the way sometimes this gets built."],[78.0,"Any any criticism of the wealthy in our modern society often gets billed as, oh, well, this is jealousy, this is envy."],[88.56,"But chapter five here comes after chapter four that we just read."],[95.6,"Right? So St."],[97.12,"James is not now contradicting himself and and teaching envy."],[102.48,"So how how did you get rich at that time in history?"],[106.56,"Were there a lot of people getting rich by not participating in the world system that existed at that time?"],[115.84,"Right. Yeah, you didn't go and and work hard as a fisherman in Galilee and become incredibly wealthy."],[119.84,"Right. That did not happen."],[123.8,"Field work was not going."],[125.8,"Working with your hands was not going to get you rich."],[130.76,"There's no nothing like that in this uh in the Roman, this is pre-feudal even, economic system."],[141.24,"Um that is not how one became wealthy."],[143.48,"So if someone became wealthy, you became wealthy by the means described above, by wedding yourself to the world, by doing things the way the world does things."],[156.68,"By collaborating with evil, essentially."],[160.52,"Okay. And I'm not saying that to say, well, so now it's fine for people to be obscenely rich."],[168.28,"Right. Um, because it is not fine, as we're gonna see as we go forward."],[173.96,"This is this is something that as much as especially as Americans, we don't want to hear it."],[180.92,"And the reason we don't want to hear it is that uh we're all richer than we think we are."],[188.6,"If you go by the current world population, right, poor people in America are fairly wealthy."],[197.56,"If you go by historically, humans who have lived in the history of the world, we're obscenely wealthy compared to the way most people have lived throughout time, but even in the world, just a cross-section of the world today, we're all doing very well."],[214.6,"So we all kind of realize that, you know, me just pointing out, oh, see, this is going after Jeff Bezos."],[224.08,"Right. So this is pointing a finger at all of us."],[230.48,"Um, but also whatever we want to say about someone who has accumulated a lot of money and what they've done with it."],[242.64,"More important for every one of us is uh the desire to accumulate wealth, which is inculcated in us by society."],[254.08,"Not only that that kind of ambition, right, the the ambition for wealth and prestige is a good thing, and someone who doesn't have it is sort of lazy and wicked."],[269.6,"Uh, but that uh then acquiring that, being successful at acquiring wealth and prestige is a marker of virtue."],[279.52,"Right? This necessarily means you know that that someone who is poor is uh poor because they're sinful and wicked, and someone who's wealthy is wealthy because they're virtuous in a Christian sense."],[293.28,"Um in a Christian sense."],[296.0,"And this is a problem that is not unique to contemporary America because of late capitalism or something."],[301.92,"This goes all the way back."],[303.2,"St. John Chrysostom, the beginning of the fifth century, was having to speak to the people in Constantinople, the capital of a chunk of the world at the time, uh, that uh that uh when they judged the poor rather than using their wealth to help them that they were sinning against Christ."],[325.92,"Okay, so this this goes a long way back."],[328.48,"This has nothing to do with any particular economic system."],[329.84,"This even happens in the book of Job."],[331.8,"This is something to which humans are tempted."],[336.44,"When Job loses all of his wealth and everything, his friends come to him and say, Oh, you must have done something really evil and sinful, that God took all that away from you."],[346.28,"Right. So this is this is something that's that's constant for all of us."],[353.56,"And so third and final piece before we go on, we also always have to remember, and I said this back in the Gospels."],[364.92,"I pointed out we have these long passages in the Gospels where Christ is railing against the Pharisees and calling them hypocrites and vipers and whitewashed tombs and you know, you cross heaven and earth to make a convert, you make him twice the son of hell, you are your sons under your father, the devil."],[384.52,"Lots of unchrist-like things Christ says to them."],[387.56,"Really me. But he says all these things, and and sometimes we're kind of aghast at that, you know, that he's he's talking that way, like I just made that joke."],[398.2,"He's talking that way to these people."],[400.12,"But we have to remember he's still talking to them."],[405.0,"Right? If Christ didn't care about them and their salvation, he wouldn't be railing against them, he wouldn't talk to them at all."],[412.2,"He'd just keep walking."],[414.84,"Right. So that the way his his rhetoric toward them escalates over the course of his ministry, as we read it in the gospels, is because he's having to sort of hit harder and harder to try to get through to them."],[431.96,"To to repent."],[434.04,"To get them to come back."],[434.84,"So when we read St."],[438.64,"James using strong language like this, he's not doing this to judge them and condemn them and saying, throw all the wealthy people out of your church, because all wealthy people are evil."],[450.24,"He's speaking to the wealthy people."],[453.6,"To try to get bring them to repentance before it's too late by pointing out, as we're gonna see, the dangers, the judgment that's gonna come with that, with that wealth."],[466.56,"So and he's he's really in this first verse inverting our expectations in a way similar to what Christ does uh in the Beatitudes, especially the way they are in St."],[477.76,"Luke's gospel, where it also has the woes."],[480.88,"He says, Blessed are the poor."],[484.16,"And then he says, Woe to you who are rich."],[488.32,"Well, that's directly counter to that instinct we have."],[493.68,"We would say, well, obviously the rich are blessed, the rich have been blessed by God with their wealth."],[499.76,"And the poor, woe to the poor, the poor are cursed for some reason."],[504.48,"So this is inverting that again, and that's the same thing St."],[507.52,"James does. He's saying, You people who are wealthy."],[512.32,"And what did he just say?"],[513.44,"He just used this mourning and weeping to talk about humility, humbling ourselves before God and repenting."],[520.8,"So he's saying, You who are rich, you shouldn't be celebrating and throwing the big parties, right, and rolling out the barrel and enjoying your wealth."],[529.44,"You should be weeping and howling because of what's coming."],[535.36,"Right. Because of what's coming."],[537.12,"Verse 2 Your riches are corrupted, and your garments are moth eaten."],[539.52,"Your gold and silver are corroded, and their corrosion will be a witness against you, and will eat your flesh like fire."],[550.44,"You have heaped up treasure in the last days."],[554.84,"Okay. So yeah, there's some St."],[560.12,"John the Forerunner there, too."],[562.04,"Right? St. John the Baptist."],[564.2,"Right. Um, we just read it because we just had Theophany."],[568.44,"Uh, but uh that's one of my favorite."],[571.24,"That's that's how uh St."],[573.24,"John the Baptist begins his gospel presentation is you brood of vipers who warned you to flee from the wrath that's coming."],[581.64,"Like that's a great opening line for people you're trying to evangelize."],[585.48,"Uh um, yeah."],[588.92,"And so remember, we talked about the last days."],[593.0,"That this is referring to sort of the last age, the messianic age."],[597.32,"So the Messiah has come, now there is, as was as we find in the prophets of the Old Testament, the Messiah will come, and then there will be this messianic age, and then there will be the end."],[608.2,"And so this is why over and over again we see in St."],[611.96,"Paul and in the general epistles, these are the last days."],[615.4,"Over and over again, these last days."],[617.24,"Meaning just that we're not that there's only days left before Christ returns, but we're in this last period."],[624.44,"And so he's saying, We're in this last period, last period before what?"],[629.0,"Before the judgment, before Christ returns to judge the living and the dead, and you're heaping up treasures for yourself on earth."],[637.96,"How is that a good idea?"],[640.2,"Right? Remember what Christ said."],[642.84,"Sorry for yourselves."],[644.24,"There's it everywhere, moth and rust do not destroy."],[644.92,"What was the language St."],[648.32,"James just used?"],[650.16,"Moth-eaten robes, corroded."],[653.12,"Gold and silver, this is that."],[654.72,"Resonance again with Christ's Christ's preaching."],[659.04,"So this judgment is coming."],[661.76,"And remember, we've talked about how justice is everything being in its correct place."],[667.44,"And that when judgment happens, that's balancing the scales, bringing things back into balance."],[673.92,"So there are people on earth who are wicked who have prospered, and people on earth who have followed God, have followed Christ, and who have suffered."],[684.08,"And when Christ returns, he's going to balance those scales."],[688.08,"So if you're one of the people who suffered unjustly, then Christ judging the earth is good news for you."],[695.04,"If you are the people who's prospered."],[698.16,"And who's followed your own pleasures and is he spent your life heaping up treasures for yourself on earth, then you're gonna have the come down."],[707.28,"Right? And so that's what he's saying to the rich."],[710.0,"He's saying, you've spent your life rather than following Christ and seeking faithfulness to Christ and keeping the commandments, doing the good."],[717.6,"Remember where we just came from."],[719.44,"You see good you can do, and you're not doing it."],[722.56,"Right? If you're a wealthy person, you now have the ability because of your wealth to do a lot of good."],[730.0,"And if instead of that you're just sort of enjoying your wealth and you're not doing it, what does that mean?"],[736.56,"Sit. Right? And so this wealth is all going to testify against you."],[743.48,"When the time comes."],[745.44,"So he's saying the time's short, and you got a lot of work to do."],[749.84,"You need to change now."],[750.52,"Right? That's why he's hitting them now."],[753.56,"Right. You need to head in the other direction now."],[761.32,"Verse 4, indeed, the wages of the laborers who mowed your fields, which you kept back by fraud, cry out."],[768.84,"And the cries of the reapers have reached the ears of the Lord of Sabo."],[774.92,"So this is not just, oh, all of you have done this one bad thing."],[779.96,"You haven't paid the guys who work for you."],[782.28,"This is this is an example."],[784.92,"Right? This is an example."],[787.64,"Right. And it's an example that illustrates what?"],[791.4,"That illustrates you've become wealthy."],[795.16,"You've accumulated on the backs of the work of others."],[801.08,"Right? They've worked and they've suffered."],[803.72,"They haven't been paid."],[805.0,"They can't feed their families."],[806.76,"They can't provide for themselves."],[809.24,"Because you've been seeking to enrich yourself and not to help them."],[813.56,"Not to do the good that you could do with that wealth to help them."],[818.36,"Right? You could be helping the whole community."],[820.84,"Right? You could be lifting up the whole community with your wealth, and instead you're keeping it for yourself."],[829.24,"And he's saying when that happens, right, those people are crying out to God for judgment, for relief."],[840.2,"Right? The people who've been oppressed, the people who've been treated unfairly, the people who have suffered."],[845.24,"They're praying day and night and asking God for help."],[849.72,"Right? And they're praying against you."],[852.32,"Say James is saying that should worry you a lot."],[856.96,"Right. And so what you should be wanting to do is make that right now."],[864.48,"Right? Christ said something similar in uh again in St."],[869.68,"Matthew's Gospel. He says, if someone's taking you to court and you're smart, you'll talk to them on the way and work it out before you get to the judge."],[880.64,"Right? It's the same kind of idea St."],[882.8,"James is doing here."],[884.0,"You need to fix this now before you stand in front of the judge."],[890.0,"Right. Verse 5, you have lived on the earth in pleasure and luxury."],[897.36,"You have fattened your hearts as in a day of slaughter."],[902.48,"So say you're living in all this luxury."],[907.2,"They say you're getting fat."],[909.44,"Because you have all this fine food and everything."],[912.4,"And you're living in all this luxury."],[914.4,"He says, and what what did they do in that day if they were going to throw a big party?"],[919.6,"Well, they'd get one of their animals and they try and fatten it up so that they can kill it and eat it."],[926.08,"Right? St. James is saying, yeah, you're fattening yourself up, and the slaughter's coming."],[933.52,"Right. Verse 6, you have condemned, you have murdered the just."],[939.6,"He does not resist you."],[942.24,"Right? So you've been taking advantage of other people who are more righteous than you."],[948.64,"And because they're more righteous than you, they haven't taken revenge."],[953.44,"Right? They haven't, they haven't tried to revolt against you."],[956.64,"They haven't tried to take what you should be giving them."],[960.6,"Right. And you've been using that to your advantage too."],[964.6,"Right? You've been using that to your advantage too."],[966.84,"And there's this reckoning coming when this is going to be straightened out."],[974.84,"Verse 7, therefore be patient, brethren, until the coming of the Lord."],[980.2,"See how the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, waiting patiently for it until it receives the early and latter rain."],[987.56,"You also be patient."],[989.24,"Establish your hearts, for the coming of the Lord is at hand."],[994.04,"So this is now talking to the other group."],[997.64,"Right. Right? Because what he's not, he's making clear what he's not saying is, hey, they're taking it this these wealthy people are taking advantage of you."],[1007.64,"You need to go get what's yours."],[1011.0,"Right? He's not saying that."],[1013.24,"He's saying, just as he was warning them, hey, Christ is going to return and he's going to sort this out."],[1019.08,"He's now telling the other group, the people who are being oppressed, the people who are suffering, saying, Hey, Christ is coming and he's going to sort this out."],[1027.0,"Right. And you need to be patient until then."],[1029.48,"You need to not try to take this into your own hands."],[1033.24,"Right? It's you're not not your job to go take try and take revenge or try to seize what should be your."],[1039.96,"Even if you're right."],[1041.64,"Even if you're right, then it should be yours."],[1044.36,"Right? You don't take that into your own hands."],[1046.76,"Be patient and wait for Christ."],[1049.16,"And you worry about, as he says, um he uses this example."],[1055.32,"He talked where we talked about fruits in terms of the works produced by faithfulness."],[1060.12,"Right? He says, the farmer waits, he's patient, and then the fruit comes."],[1064.96,"And so if you're patient, then when Christ comes, your reward will come."],[1069.04,"And the reward that will come with Christ is going to be better than what you could go steal from him."],[1075.04,"Right. However it is you've been wronged on this earth."],[1080.08,"What Christ is going to offer you is going to be better than all those riches he's hoarding and all of the fine foods he's eating and the big house he lives in and all of that."],[1089.36,"Right. Verse 9, do not grump grumble against one another, brethren, lest you be condemned."],[1099.84,"Behold, the judge is standing at the door."],[1103.2,"So he's saying, being patient also means you don't sit around and grumble."],[1110.0,"Right. So sit around complaining, that no good."],[1114.32,"He's all like man, chip danger, game."],[1116.32,"Right. That's not being patient."],[1119.52,"And waiting for the Lord."],[1121.36,"Right. So he's saying, if you if you're doing that, because remember."],[1126.08,"Just like hating someone is like murdering them."],[1130.8,"Or I didn't go and try and get revenge, but a man, I sat around complaining and badmouthing him every day."],[1136.48,"That's you're still subject to condemnation."],[1142.64,"My brethren, take the prophets who spoke in the name of the Lord as an example of suffering and patience."],[1150.32,"Indeed, we count them blessed who endured."],[1152.88,"You have heard of the perseverance of Job, and seeing the end intended by the Lord, that the Lord is very compassionate and merciful."],[1161.72,"Right? So he gives them examples."],[1163.84,"He says, Go back to the prophets."],[1165.28,"How many of the prophets?"],[1167.04,"I mean, pick one Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel."],[1169.68,"They were faithful and served the Lord Elijah."],[1171.0,"They came and spoke the word of the Lord."],[1176.2,"Right, Elisha. They did not have a good time."],[1180.92,"Right? They were they were hated, rejected, persecuted, lied about."],[1186.92,"But they remained faithful."],[1189.32,"Right? See the reward they received."],[1191.8,"Then he points to Job."],[1193.24,"Look at everything Job went through."],[1195.32,"He suffered all that unjustly, and everyone accused him."],[1198.92,"His wife told him to curse God and die."],[1201.96,"Right? Like, I mean, that's his quote unquote friends all condemned him and accused him."],[1208.92,"But he remained faithful."],[1211.4,"To God and endured suffering, and at the end, right, God was faithful and rewarded him."],[1218.2,"So he's saying, look at these examples."],[1224.12,"Verse 12, but above all, my brethren, do not swear, either by heaven or by earth or with any other oath, but let your yes be yes and your no, lest you fall into judgment."],[1236.92,"That's almost a direct quote from Christ in St."],[1240.28,"Matthew's gospel. Um the idea here is that is not, again, to make this legalistic rule."],[1250.6,"Oh, don't ever swear an oath."],[1252.12,"If they take you to if you go to court and demand an exception."],[1255.8,"The point is that you should be honest and tell the truth."],[1261.24,"That's what it means for your yes to be yes and your no to be no."],[1264.52,"That you just you tell the truth."],[1266.68,"And so you don't have to be, I swear on my mother's grave and on my children's heads."],[1275.12,"Because someone who has to do that in order to get people to believe them is obviously someone who doesn't usually tell the truth."],[1284.32,"And isn't trustworthy."],[1288.72,"Yeah. So part of being faithful is being truthful and being honest."],[1294.0,"And being straightforward."],[1301.6,"Verse 13, is anyone among you suffering?"],[1304.88,"Let him pray."],[1306.64,"Is anyone cheerful?"],[1307.92,"Let him sing psalms."],[1310.56,"Right. So some of you are suffering, in that case, pray."],[1315.84,"Right? For God that God will give you patience for endurance."],[1319.52,"Are you cheerful?"],[1320.16,"Are things going well?"],[1321.2,"Are you are feeling blessed?"],[1323.92,"Then sing psalms, meaning give praise back to God."],[1327.28,"Give praise and thanks to God for what you have that's making you happy, which is not always what we do."],[1334.72,"When we're suffering, a lot of us will pray when we're in trouble."],[1338.56,"But a lot of times when we're cheerful, when we're joyful, uh we kind of forget about God."],[1344.48,"That's why those two go together."],[1347.84,"Verse 14 is anyone among you sick, let him call for the elders of the church and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord."],[1358.88,"And the prayer of faith will save the sick, and the Lord will raise him up, and if he has committed sins, he will be forgiven."],[1368.64,"Um, as we've talked about before, faith is pretty much always better translated, faithfulness."],[1374.72,"And this is another case where it makes more sense."],[1377.12,"The prayer of faithfulness."],[1379.16,"Right? Which makes sense in the context of what he talked about in terms of asking and how you ask."],[1385.4,"Right? The prayer that we pray in faithfulness to God."],[1390.2,"Is the one that God is going to answer the affirmative."],[1394.28,"Not the one where we're praying based on our own desires and uh sinful sinful pleasures."],[1402.36,"But this is where we get the sacrament or the holy mystery of the anointing of the sick."],[1409.32,"Right. This is what St."],[1411.8,"James and what Christ through the scriptures tells us to do if someone is uh is sick."],[1419.56,"And notice the two pieces, they're always put together."],[1424.28,"And that's healing from sickness and the forgiveness of sins."],[1427.88,"Healing of body and soul."],[1430.76,"Right, as we pray when we perform."],[1433.56,"Because these two things are always connected."],[1438.04,"Contradict heart. We are a human being is an ensolved body, because soul really just means life."],[1450.44,"Right? So if there's no soul, if there's no life, then it's a dead body."],[1454.68,"It's an object and it's going to decay."],[1458.12,"Right? So we are a human is both of those."],[1462.44,"And so if something's wrong with one of those, it's going to affect the other."],[1469.08,"Right. Because we're a we're a whole person."],[1476.84,"We're 16 confess your trespasses to one another and pray for one another that you may be healed."],[1483.64,"The effective, fervent prayer of a righteous man avails much."],[1488.88,"So this little piece here, this one verse is summarizing most of in St."],[1496.72,"Matthew's gospel, chapters 17, 18, and part of 19."],[1501.2,"Uh, we won't go back through all that, obviously, right now, but um, that's where Christ talks about how sin is dealt with in the church and how he has given the church as a body, the community, the ability to pray for one another and repent collectively, and that forgiveness of sins comes through that."],[1525.52,"God can forgive sins however he wants."],[1528.64,"He can do it through water baptism, he could do it through the prayers of the community."],[1534.32,"And so St."],[1534.96,"James sort of is summarizing that idea here very briefly that you confess to each other your sins and you pray for each other, and then you are healed."],[1546.72,"And he makes very clear, because the further prayers of a of a righteous man are powerful and effective."],[1554.32,"That's connected right here to the forgiveness of sins."],[1556.8,"Okay, and this is where the sacrament or the holy mystery of confession."],[1563.28,"It doesn't come from this verse in St."],[1564.96,"James, but this idea that's being summarized here that's also Christ talks about in St."],[1569.28,"Matthew's gospel, because remember, in the early church, people confess to the whole community."],[1578.2,"Right. There are benefits to that."],[1584.32,"I'm not saying we're ever gonna go back to that, because I don't think we're ever gonna go back to that."],[1590.12,"There are benefits to that because I think we are, especially in terms of the sins that we're struggling with, far too closed off from each other."],[1599.64,"We desperately try to keep that a secret."],[1602.84,"And because as someone who hears confessions, everybody thinks that they're the most twisted, evil pervert in the world."],[1611.88,"Everyone does. And the reality is none of us really is."],[1619.32,"We're all struggling."],[1620.52,"In a lot of cases, you know, we're all struggling with the same things, but we don't feel like we can ever admit that to each other."],[1628.84,"And share that with each other and help each other and pray with each other."],[1631.96,"And so there was a great benefit to being able to be open and honest, right, with your church family, with your community about what you were struggling with, so they could support you."],[1642.36,"Right? Not so they could gossip about it and point fingers, because they were also being open about what they were struggling with."],[1648.52,"Right? So this was this was not like, you know, oh, someone found out your secret."],[1653.48,"This was more like an AA meeting."],[1655.24,"Right? This is where we're all just putting on the table, hey, we're all broken, we're all struggling, we're all dealing with different shades of different things, but a lot of the same stuff."],[1665.4,"Let's support each other and help each other."],[1667.16,"So there were real benefits to that."],[1669.8,"Like I said, I don't think we're ever going back to that."],[1672.68,"But that is still how confession works in the Orthodox Church."],[1677.0,"It's just now we have the priest there as the representative of the congregation."],[1684.6,"That's why when you come to confession here, you don't confess to me, you confess to Christ."],[1691.24,"And I'm standing there with you."],[1693.68,"Right as a witness."],[1694.6,"I'm there representing the community."],[1694.84,"In terms of how that's done."],[1698.32,"Right. Because God mediates that forgiveness through the community."],[1703.68,"Because again, forgiveness is not you did this bad thing, this is the punishment for it."],[1710.64,"We're going to let you off the hook."],[1712.24,"That's not forgiveness."],[1714.8,"Forgiveness is actual healing and restoration."],[1720.08,"And that has to take place in terms of a community."],[1723.92,"Because the sins I commit do not just affect me."],[1729.68,"The sins I commit affect my wife."],[1733.12,"They even affect my dogs."],[1736.08,"If I yell at them."],[1738.24,"They affect, right, everything."],[1740.16,"They affect the church."],[1743.52,"Right? Because the mood I'm in and what I'm doing and if I'm struggling and all this, it affects everything around me."],[1751.04,"So it's not a question of being left off the hook."],[1753.52,"I have to be healed from that sin."],[1756.72,"And I have to restore everything else."],[1760.24,"Right? I have to take care of all the effects."],[1762.4,"I have to clean up all the mess."],[1764.72,"That's what forgiveness is."],[1774.64,"Matthew's gospel, as we mentioned, centered around chapter 18."],[1778.72,"And what St."],[1781.6,"James is talking about here."],[1784.08,"Right? That God does have the power to clean up that mess, but he does it within within the community, because sin takes place within the community."],[1797.92,"Verse 17. Elijah was a man with a nature like ours, and he prayed earnestly that it would not rain, and it did not rain on the land for three years and six months."],[1809.16,"And he prayed again, and the heaven gave rain, and the earth produced its fruit."],[1813.8,"Right? So he's saying Prophet Elijah, at the time we're talking about, right, was not some kind of mystical being."],[1822.92,"He didn't have any superpowers."],[1825.0,"He's he's a human like us."],[1827.96,"But he was faithful and following God, and he prayed that it wouldn't rain and it didn't rain for three and a half years."],[1834.04,"And then when he prayed that it would rain, it did, and the earth brought forth its fruit."],[1838.76,"Why is he using that particular example?"],[1843.08,"Well, let's go back to St."],[1846.2,"Matthew's gospel. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven."],[1850.76,"What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven."],[1855.4,"Right? And so this is literally in the heavens in the sky."],[1860.36,"The prophet Elijah bound it and then he loosed it."],[1863.72,"Right? And the rain when it came represents this healing and this new life."],[1869.88,"Right. So this is an example of that kind of binding and loosing that then he's applying to the forgiveness of sins."],[1879.56,"Right within the community."],[1882.12,"Right? And that our prayers, the binding, by the way, if you're wondering what that would be, that would be like what St."],[1890.12,"Paul talks about with excommunication."],[1892.6,"It's not condemning someone."],[1895.8,"But it's saying we're gonna shut you off from the community in order to bring you to repentance."],[1903.56,"Right? It's imposing repentance needs to take place here before the restoration."],[1910.96,"Right. And then the rain coming, the loosing, right, is that restoration."],[1918.0,"Sometimes there has to be a period of separation before the restoration."],[1922.96,"Sometimes it's just the restoration."],[1925.44,"Right? That comes immediately."],[1927.44,"But that restoration happens in the church through the prayers of the church."],[1940.8,"And finally, as we come to the end of the epistle, brethren, if anyone among you wanders from the truth and someone turns him back, let him know that he who turns a sinner from the error of his way will save a soul from death and cover a multitude of sins."],[1959.68,"Okay. So yes, that's kind of an abrupt ending."],[1966.08,"Right. But as we said at the beginning of St."],[1968.72,"James's Epistle, he doesn't follow the exact same format as St."],[1971.92,"Paul. We saw it had a much briefer introduction."],[1975.2,"It also has a sort of more sudden and abrupt uh conclusion."],[1980.64,"And that's that's as we talked about, that's probably mostly because it wasn't just said to one particular person the way St."],[1988.64,"Paul's work. So if St."],[1990.72,"James is writing this letter to circulate through several Christian communities, he's not necessarily going to identify particular people."],[1998.72,"Because it doesn't just have one addressee."],[2001.84,"But so this last statement he makes."],[2005.6,"The interesting part is the end of it."],[2007.6,"The first part would seem obvious to us."],[2009.08,"Like, yeah, that's a."],[2010.28,"If someone's turned to sin, or if they've turned away from the faith, and you go and you help them and help them come back and restore them, obviously that's a good thing to do."],[2019.0,"So that part's more clear."],[2021.88,"But the interesting part is at the end, he'll save a soul from death."],[2027.48,"Talking about the person."],[2030.6,"Um, brief note here."],[2033.08,"Um, I know, and I know why, it's bred into people in our our largely Protestant culture."],[2040.84,"When we say most holy Theotokos save us, there's a whole bunch of people who flinch and blanch."],[2047.08,"Because they want to say that word save only applies to God."],[2050.6,"Well, I've pointed out a few other places."],[2052.6,"Here's another one where St."],[2055.48,"James says, if you help bring that person restore them, then you have saved a soul from death."],[2060.36,"Now he's not saying Christ wasn't involved."],[2065.08,"He's not saying, right, that you save people the way Christ saves people, but what?"],[2070.76,"But you've been the means, you've been the tool, you've been what Christ used to bring that person back and to save them."],[2078.6,"It's Christ who's doing the same, but he's doing it through us through you in this case."],[2083.72,"Just like St."],[2084.92,"Paul talked about, you know, I've become all things who are all men, so that by all means I might save some, or St."],[2092.84,"Paul taking credit for saving people."],[2095.08,"No. Right? He's saying he wants to do everything he can to be the means through which."],[2101.32,"By him preaching the gospel to someone."],[2104.6,"That his preaching, his faithfulness to Christ would be the means by which Christ saves that person."],[2111.08,"Right? Christ doesn't need Saint Paul."],[2113.84,"Right? St. James isn't saying that Christ needs me to go find that person, or else they're gonna be eternally condemned."],[2122.4,"They're gonna go to hell if I don't go out and say that."],[2124.64,"No, Christ doesn't need me."],[2126.88,"Right. But if I go and do that, then I make myself the means by which I offer myself to Christ, as a tool through which for Christ to do this."],[2141.44,"So the soul is safe from death, and that has a benefit for me, as we've talked about."],[2146.64,"We talked about this is so our understanding in the Orthodox Church of synergy."],[2151.76,"Is that we allow God to work through us."],[2155.52,"God, one of God's energies, one of the ways he works in the world is by loving his creation and loving the people in it."],[2163.44,"When we allow God to love people through us, right, that transforms us."],[2169.12,"That changes us, that has an effect on us."],[2172.4,"Right. God is working to bring peace in the world when we are a peacemaker, when we go and work to make peace, then is God working through us, right, to do that."],[2183.12,"He gets the credit."],[2184.0,"It's him doing it."],[2185.92,"The reason those are good works and God calls them good is because they're his works."],[2190.48,"Right? They're done through us through us."],[2193.84,"This is the same idea here."],[2195.92,"He says, if God, if you do this and God saves a person through you, what is the effect on you?"],[2202.88,"It covers a multitude of sins."],[2205.12,"This is atonement language."],[2208.32,"Okay. The word that's translated atone."],[2212.64,"Atonement is a made-up English word."],[2215.76,"Okay, it does not pre-exist the translation of the Bible."],[2219.04,"Wycliffe made it up."],[2219.92,"It was at one mint."],[2221.08,"And he had it hyphenated."],[2226.44,"That's literally being made at one again."],[2229.24,"That was his idea."],[2230.36,"And so he just coined the term at one mint."],[2233.96,"Right? You've been reunified at one."],[2238.28,"But the word in Hebrew, the the uh kefer verbs, right, which that's where like Yom Kippur, the day of atonement."],[2248.52,"Uh is from that same word group, literally means cover."],[2253.64,"Right? To cover, or it can mean to purify."],[2257.8,"Or to wipe or to smear something, like over the top of something to cover it."],[2262.76,"Um those are sort of the ranges of meaning."],[2265.4,"So this cover language."],[2267.88,"We're gonna see this again in in St."],[2270.04,"Peter's epistles that we're gonna go into next."],[2271.96,"He's gonna use the same kind of language about sins."],[2274.92,"Um the idea is that it that it covers over."],[2278.28,"So this is this is atonement language, and this is a way of using language that's in here in the New Testament."],[2285.32,"We're gonna see more of it, as I said in in 1 Peter, and we'll talk about it more there."],[2290.12,"Um, but this is very common in the church fathers, too."],[2295.0,"And it's not in church fathers who we read a lot in the West, because the West hasn't been big fans of this particular bit of early Christian theology, because again, the atonement discussions in the West come to surround Christ and punishment and and all of these things that aren't necessarily there in the scriptures."],[2320.0,"And so the idea of of God doing something through us and that having the effect of atoning for our sins."],[2332.08,"Bothers a lot of sensibilities."],[2334.64,"Right. But how are we just describing the kind of synergy that we talk about?"],[2342.8,"Right? Is that it changes us."],[2346.08,"Right. So when we allow ourselves to be the means through which God works in other people's lives to bring them back to faith, to bring them to repentance, that has an effect in us."],[2356.4,"And what did we just say forgiveness was?"],[2360.32,"Forgiveness is actual healing."],[2363.76,"Actual cleansing, actual purification."],[2367.6,"Right. This is how this is tied to what we were just talking about about the forgiveness of sins."],[2374.24,"Right? If you want to experience the forgiveness of sins."],[2378.88,"The forgiveness of your sins."],[2380.96,"That being covered over of that being being purified, be the means through which someone else comes to repentance."],[2389.52,"And then you experience the forgiveness that you're seeking for the sins that you're repenting of."],[2421.68,"If you want to experience peace in your life, then go and be a peacemaker."],[2427.52,"Right. All and this is true across the board."],[2432.2,"Right. And this is, we talked about this a little, but this is just a particular of what St."],[2438.36,"Paul was talking about in Hebrews when he said that faith, and we said should be faithfulness is the substance of things hoped for."],[2450.44,"Right? The things we're hoping for, the things God has promised us that we want to receive, those become substantial to us."],[2458.28,"Those become real to us when we're faithful."],[2462.84,"When we're faithful to Christ and following Him and doing those things which He's commanded us to do."],[2476.12,"So that's where St."],[2477.16,"James's epistle ends."],[2480.36,"Next time, uh, Lord willing, next week, um, we'll be starting 1 Peter."],[2489.72,"So we'll have an introduction to the epistles of St."],[2494.68,"Peter, and also Jude."],[2498.28,"So this is the first time in going through the entire Bible that I'm going to go out of order."],[2503.16,"Um, but uh 2 Peter and Jude have a very interesting relationship to each other, even though in our current New Testaments, first, second, and third John are in between."],[2517.24,"Uh, there's a very special and peculiar relationship that we'll talk more about next time between 2 Peter and Jude."],[2524.36,"And in fact, the relationship between the two of them was one of the questions on my doctoral comprehensive exams."],[2531.0,"So I've had to put a lot of thought into it, and I'm going to inflict it on all of you."],[2545.36,"John, and then what we'll have left is Revelation in the New Testament."],[2549.84,"So thank you, everybody."],[2553.12,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole council of God."],[2560.48,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2565.84,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.64,"lv":2,"h":"5:1 - Woe to the rich: not class envy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:1</b> \"Come now, you rich, weep and howl\" - \"this is gonna offend some people, but hey, it's St. James\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"We take everything in the Bible <b>literally</b>\" - until <b>sell everything, give to the poor</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suddenly \"all the reasons why that doesn't apply to me\" - \"here we go again\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not class envy</b> - any criticism of the wealthy gets billed as <b>jealousy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ch. 5 comes after <b>ch. 4</b> - James not contradicting himself, not teaching envy</li><li class=\"d0\">How did you get rich in that era?</li><li class=\"d1\">not by working hard as a <b>fisherman in Galilee</b> - \"that did not happen\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>field work</b>, working with hands, not going to make you rich; <b>pre-feudal</b> Roman economy</li><li class=\"d1\">you got wealthy \"by <b>wedding yourself to the world</b>\", doing things the world's way</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>collaborating with evil</b>, essentially\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not saying it's fine to be <b>obscenely rich</b> - \"it is not fine, as we're gonna see\"</li></ul>"},{"t":160.52,"lv":3,"h":"We are all richer than we think","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Americans especially \"<b>don't want to hear it</b>\" - because \"<b>we're all richer than we think</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">By current world population, <b>poor people in America</b> are fairly wealthy</li><li class=\"d0\">Historically, all humans ever: \"<b>obscenely wealthy</b>\" compared to how most have lived</li><li class=\"d0\">Even a cross-section of the world today: \"we're all doing very well\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So not just \"going after <b>Jeff Bezos</b>\" - \"pointing a finger at <b>all of us</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Whatever we say about someone who accumulated money and what they did with it - <b>more important for each of us</b>:</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>desire to accumulate wealth</b>, \"inculcated in us by society\"</li></ul>"},{"t":242.64,"lv":3,"h":"Wealth as a marker of virtue","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Society's message: <b>ambition for wealth and prestige</b> is good</li><li class=\"d1\">someone without it is \"sort of <b>lazy and wicked</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Success at acquiring = <b>marker of virtue</b></li><li class=\"d1\">poor = poor because <b>sinful and wicked</b>; wealthy = wealthy because <b>virtuous</b> \"in a Christian sense\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not unique to \"late capitalism or something\" - goes <b>all the way back</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. John Chrysostom</b>, early 5th c., <b>Constantinople</b>: people judged the poor instead of using wealth to help them</li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>sinning against Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Nothing to do with any economic system - \"something to which <b>humans are tempted</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Job</b>: loses everything, friends say \"you must have done something really evil\" - \"constant for all of us\"</li></ul>"},{"t":353.56,"lv":3,"h":"Christ's harsh rhetoric still aims at repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Third piece, from the Gospels: Christ railing at the <b>Pharisees</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hypocrites, vipers, <b>whitewashed tombs</b>, \"make him twice the son of hell\", \"your father the devil\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"lots of <b>unchrist-like</b> things Christ says\" - we're aghast</li><li class=\"d0\">But \"he's <b>still talking to them</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if he didn't care about their salvation \"he'd just <b>keep walking</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">rhetoric escalates over his ministry - hitting <b>harder and harder</b> to get through → <b>repent</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So James's strong language: <b>not</b> \"throw all the wealthy out of your church\"</li><li class=\"d1\">speaking <b>to</b> the wealthy to bring them to repentance <b>before it's too late</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First verse <b>inverts our expectations</b> like Christ's <b>Beatitudes</b> (esp. <b>Luke</b>, with the <b>woes</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Blessed are the poor\" / \"<b>Woe to you who are rich</b>\" - against our instinct: rich = blessed by God, poor = cursed</li><li class=\"d1\">just used mourning/weeping for <b>humility</b>, repenting → rich shouldn't be \"<b>rolling out the barrel</b>\", but <b>weeping and howling</b> for what's coming</li></ul>"},{"t":537.12,"lv":2,"h":"5:2-3 - Corroded riches, last days","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:2-3</b> riches corrupted, garments <b>moth-eaten</b>, gold and silver <b>corroded</b></li><li class=\"d1\">corrosion = <b>witness against you</b>, \"will eat your flesh like fire\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you have <b>heaped up treasure in the last days</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Some <b>St. John the Forerunner</b> there, too\" - read it just after <b>Theophany</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his gospel presentation: \"<b>brood of vipers</b>, who warned you to flee the wrath\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"great opening line for people you're trying to evangelize\"</li></ul>"},{"t":588.92,"lv":3,"h":"Last days, judgment, balancing the scales","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Last days</b> = the last age, the <b>messianic age</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophets: Messiah comes → messianic age → <b>the end</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why Paul and the general epistles keep saying \"these are the last days\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"only days left\" - we're in the <b>last period before judgment</b>, Christ returning to judge living and dead</li><li class=\"d0\">Heaping up treasure on earth - \"how is that a good idea?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">echo of Christ: <b>moth and rust</b> do not destroy; James's moth-eaten robes, corroded gold</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Justice</b> = everything in its correct place; <b>judgment = balancing the scales</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wicked who prospered vs. those who followed Christ and <b>suffered</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffered unjustly → judgment is <b>good news</b>; lived for own pleasures → \"you're gonna have the <b>come down</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Remember <b>ch. 4</b>: you see good you can do and don't do it</li><li class=\"d1\">wealth = ability to do a lot of good; just enjoying it → \"what does that mean?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wealth \"all going to <b>testify against you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Time's short, you got a lot of work to do\" - <b>change now</b>, head the other direction</li></ul>"},{"t":761.32,"lv":2,"h":"5:4 - Wages kept back by fraud","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:4</b> wages of laborers who mowed your fields, <b>kept back by fraud</b>, cry out</li><li class=\"d1\">cries of the reapers reached the ears of the <b>Lord of Sabaoth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"you did this one bad thing\" - it's <b>an example</b></li><li class=\"d1\">illustrates: you accumulated \"<b>on the backs of the work of others</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they worked, suffered, weren't paid - \"can't feed their families\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Seeking to enrich yourself, not to <b>do the good you could</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you could be <b>lifting up the whole community</b>\" - instead keeping it for yourself</li><li class=\"d0\">The oppressed are <b>crying out to God</b> for judgment, praying \"day and night\" - \"<b>praying against you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">James: \"that should worry you a lot\" → <b>make it right now</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Like Christ in <b>Matthew</b>: taken to court, work it out <b>on the way</b> before you reach the judge</li><li class=\"d1\">\"fix this now before you stand in front of the judge\"</li></ul>"},{"t":890.0,"lv":2,"h":"5:5-6 - Fattened for the slaughter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:5</b> lived in <b>pleasure and luxury</b>, \"fattened your hearts as in a day of slaughter\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fine food, luxury → \"getting fat\"</li><li class=\"d1\">big party then: <b>fatten an animal</b> to kill and eat</li><li class=\"d1\">James: \"you're fattening yourself up, and the <b>slaughter's coming</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:6</b> \"you have condemned, <b>murdered the just</b>; he does not resist you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">taking advantage of people <b>more righteous</b> than you</li><li class=\"d1\">because they're righteous they <b>haven't taken revenge</b>, haven't revolted or taken what you should give</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you've been using that to your advantage too\"</li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>reckoning</b> is coming - it will be straightened out</li></ul>"},{"t":974.84,"lv":2,"h":"5:7-8 - Be patient until the coming","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:7</b> \"be patient, brethren, until the <b>coming of the Lord</b>\" - farmer waits for precious fruit, <b>early and latter rain</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"establish your hearts, the coming of the Lord is at hand\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Now speaking to the <b>other group</b> - the oppressed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> \"they're taking advantage of you, go get what's yours\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same message as to the rich: <b>Christ is coming and will sort this out</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Don't take it into your own hands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not your job to take revenge or seize what should be yours - \"<b>even if you're right</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"be patient and wait for Christ\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Farmer image: <b>fruit</b> = works produced by faithfulness (earlier study)</li><li class=\"d1\">patient → when Christ comes, <b>your reward comes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">better than what you could steal - better than his <b>riches, fine foods, big house</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1089.36,"lv":2,"h":"5:9 - Don't grumble","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:9</b> \"do not grumble against one another, lest you be condemned; the <b>judge is standing at the door</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Patience also means <b>no sitting around grumbling</b></li><li class=\"d1\">complaining, \"man, <b>chip on the shoulder</b>\" - not patient waiting for the Lord</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>hating someone = murdering them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't get revenge, but \"sat around <b>complaining and badmouthing him every day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">→ \"still subject to <b>condemnation</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1142.64,"lv":3,"h":"5:10-11 - Prophets and Job as examples","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:10-11</b> prophets as example of <b>suffering and patience</b>; \"we count them blessed who endured\"; <b>perseverance of Job</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prophets</b> - pick one: <b>Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Elijah, Elisha</b></li><li class=\"d1\">spoke the Lord's word, faithful - \"did not have a good time\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>hated, rejected, persecuted, lied about</b> - but remained faithful → \"see the reward\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Job</b>: suffered unjustly, everyone accused him</li><li class=\"d1\">wife: \"<b>curse God and die</b>\"; \"quote unquote friends\" condemned him</li><li class=\"d1\">remained faithful, endured → at the end <b>God was faithful and rewarded him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Look at these examples\" - the Lord is <b>compassionate and merciful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1224.12,"lv":2,"h":"5:12 - Let your yes be yes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:12</b> \"above all, do not swear\" - let your <b>yes be yes</b>, no be no, lest you fall into judgment</li><li class=\"d1\">\"almost a <b>direct quote from Christ</b>\" in Matthew</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"to make this <b>legalistic rule</b>\" - never swear an oath, even demanding an exception if taken to court</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: <b>be honest, tell the truth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no need for \"I swear on my <b>mother's grave</b> and my <b>children's heads</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">someone who must do that to be believed \"doesn't usually tell the truth\" - <b>isn't trustworthy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Part of being faithful = <b>truthful, honest, straightforward</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1301.6,"lv":2,"h":"5:13 - Suffering: pray; cheerful: sing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:13</b> suffering? <b>pray</b>. Cheerful? <b>sing psalms</b></li><li class=\"d1\">suffering → pray for God to give <b>patience for endurance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cheerful, feeling blessed → sing psalms = <b>give praise and thanks back to God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not always what we do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in trouble we pray; when cheerful, joyful \"we kind of <b>forget about God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's why those two go together\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1347.84,"lv":2,"h":"5:14-15 - Anointing of the sick","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:14</b> sick? call the <b>elders of the church</b>, pray over him, <b>anointing with oil</b> in the Lord's name</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>5:15</b> \"prayer of faith will save the sick\", Lord raises him up, sins <b>forgiven</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Faith\" nearly always better as <b>faithfulness</b> - \"the <b>prayer of faithfulness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fits his earlier teaching on <b>how you ask</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prayer prayed in faithfulness to God is the one God <b>answers in the affirmative</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not prayer from \"our own desires and <b>sinful pleasures</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Source of the <b>holy mystery of anointing of the sick</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what James and Christ \"through the scriptures\" tell us to do when someone is sick</li><li class=\"d0\">Two pieces \"always put together\": <b>healing from sickness</b> + <b>forgiveness of sins</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>healing of body and soul</b>, as in the prayers of the service</li></ul>"},{"t":1433.56,"lv":3,"h":"Body and soul are connected","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Two things \"always connected\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Human being = an <b>ensouled body</b> - <b>soul really just means life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no soul, no life → <b>dead body</b>, \"an object, going to decay\"</li><li class=\"d1\">a human is <b>both</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Something wrong with one → <b>affects the other</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we're a <b>whole person</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1476.84,"lv":2,"h":"5:16 - Confess to one another","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:16</b> \"confess your trespasses to one another, pray for one another, that you may be <b>healed</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"effective, fervent prayer of a <b>righteous man</b> avails much\"</li><li class=\"d0\">This verse <b>summarizes</b> most of <b>Matthew 17, 18, part of 19</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how <b>sin is dealt with in the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ gave the church <b>as a body, the community</b>, ability to pray for one another and <b>repent collectively</b></li><li class=\"d1\">forgiveness of sins comes through that</li><li class=\"d0\">God can forgive <b>however he wants</b></li><li class=\"d1\">through <b>water baptism</b> or <b>the prayers of the community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">James: confess sins to each other, pray for each other → <b>you are healed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">righteous man's prayer is powerful - <b>connected to forgiveness of sins</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1556.8,"lv":3,"h":"Confession in the early church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Holy mystery of confession</b> - \"doesn't come from this verse\", but from the idea summarized here (and in Matthew)</li><li class=\"d0\">Early church: people confessed <b>to the whole community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"never gonna go back to that\" - but <b>benefits</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we're \"far too <b>closed off</b> from each other\" about sins we struggle with</li><li class=\"d0\">As one who hears confessions: \"everybody thinks they're the <b>most twisted, evil pervert</b> in the world\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"none of us really is\" - all struggling, \"with the <b>same things</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't admit it to each other, share, <b>help and pray</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Openness with church family so they could <b>support</b> you</li><li class=\"d1\">not to <b>gossip</b> or point fingers - they were open too</li><li class=\"d1\">\"more like an <b>AA meeting</b>\" - \"we're all broken, all struggling\" → support each other</li></ul>"},{"t":1684.6,"lv":3,"h":"Priest as witness; forgiveness is healing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Still how confession works in the <b>Orthodox Church</b>: priest there as <b>representative of the congregation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you don't confess to me, you <b>confess to Christ</b>\" - \"I'm standing there as a <b>witness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>mediates forgiveness through the community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Forgiveness <b>isn't</b> \"here's the punishment... we'll let you off the hook\"</li><li class=\"d1\">forgiveness = <b>actual healing and restoration</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Must happen in <b>community</b> - my sins don't just affect me</li><li class=\"d1\">affect <b>my wife</b>, \"even my <b>dogs</b>, if I yell at them\", the <b>church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">my mood, my struggle → \"affects everything around me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"left off the hook\": <b>healed from the sin</b> + <b>clean up all the mess</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God has power to clean it up - but <b>within the community</b>, because sin takes place there</li><li class=\"d1\">Matthew <b>ch. 18</b> as center</li></ul>"},{"t":1797.92,"lv":2,"h":"5:17-18 - Elijah's prayer, binding and loosing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:17-18</b> Elijah \"a man with a nature like ours\" - prayed, <b>no rain 3 years 6 months</b>; prayed again, rain and fruit</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"some kind of <b>mystical being</b>\", no superpowers - \"a human like us\", faithful</li><li class=\"d0\">Why this example? Back to Matthew: <b>bind on earth, bound in heaven; loose on earth, loosed in heaven</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Elijah literally in the <b>heavens, the sky</b> - <b>bound it, then loosed it</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>rain</b> = healing and new life</li><li class=\"d1\">James applies binding and loosing to <b>forgiveness of sins</b>, within the community</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Binding</b> = like Paul's <b>excommunication</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not condemning - \"shut you off from the community to <b>bring you to repentance</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">repentance must take place <b>before restoration</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Loosing</b> = restoration (the rain)</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes a <b>period of separation</b> first; sometimes restoration comes <b>immediately</b></li><li class=\"d1\">always happens <b>in the church through the prayers of the church</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1940.8,"lv":2,"h":"5:19-20 - Turning back a sinner","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:19-20</b> if anyone wanders from the truth and someone turns him back: <b>saves a soul from death</b>, <b>covers a multitude of sins</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Kind of an <b>abrupt ending</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">unlike <b>Paul</b>: much briefer introduction, more <b>sudden conclusion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">probably because not written to <b>one person</b> - circulated through <b>several communities</b>, no single addressee</li><li class=\"d0\">First half obvious: helping someone who turned to sin or away from faith come back = <b>good thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Interesting part: \"<b>will save a soul from death</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2030.6,"lv":3,"h":"Who \"saves\"? The means, not the source","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Brief note: bred into our <b>Protestant culture</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>most holy Theotokos, save us</b>\" → people \"flinch and blanch\" - <b>save</b> only applies to God</li><li class=\"d1\">here's another place: James says you <b>saved a soul from death</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not saying Christ wasn't involved, or you save <b>the way Christ saves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you're \"the <b>means</b>, the <b>tool</b>\" Christ used - \"it's Christ who's doing\" it, through you</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>Paul</b>: \"all things to all men, that by all means I might <b>save some</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not taking credit - wants to be the means; <b>his faithfulness</b> the means by which Christ saves</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ doesn't need</b> Paul / me</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"Christ needs me to find that person or they go to hell\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if I do it, I \"offer myself to Christ as a <b>tool</b>\" - soul safe from death, and a <b>benefit for me</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2146.64,"lv":3,"h":"Synergy: God working through us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox understanding of <b>synergy</b>: we allow God to work through us</li><li class=\"d1\">one of his <b>energies</b> = <b>loving his creation</b> and the people in it</li><li class=\"d1\">God loves people through us → \"<b>transforms us</b>, changes us\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Peacemaker: God working through us to bring <b>peace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>he gets the credit</b>\" - he's doing it</li><li class=\"d1\">why they're good works: \"they're <b>his works</b>\", done through us</li><li class=\"d0\">Same idea here: God saves a person through you - effect on you?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>covers a multitude of sins</b>\" = <b>atonement language</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2205.12,"lv":3,"h":"Atonement: <i>kefer</i> = to cover","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">English <b>\"atonement\"</b> is a <b>made-up word</b> - doesn't pre-exist the Bible translation</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Wycliffe</b> coined it: <b>\"at one mint\"</b> (hyphenated) = made <b>at one again</b>, reunified</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew <i>kefer</i> verbs (source of <b>Yom Kippur</b>, day of atonement) literally = <b>to cover</b></li><li class=\"d1\">or <b>purify</b>, or <b>wipe / smear</b> over the top of something</li><li class=\"d1\">\"covers over\" - same language in <b>St. Peter's epistles</b>, next, on sins</li><li class=\"d0\">Common in the <b>church fathers</b> - but not the ones \"we read a lot in the West\"</li><li class=\"d1\">West \"not big fans\" of this early Christian theology</li><li class=\"d1\">Western atonement discussions center on <b>Christ and punishment</b> - \"not necessarily there in the scriptures\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God doing something through us that <b>atones for our sins</b> \"bothers a lot of sensibilities\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2334.64,"lv":3,"h":"Forgiveness through helping others repent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">But it's just the <b>synergy</b> already described: \"it <b>changes us</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">being the means for others to come to <b>faith and repentance</b> → effect in us</li><li class=\"d0\">Recall: forgiveness = <b>actual healing, cleansing, purification</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ties to what was said about <b>forgiveness of sins</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Want to experience forgiveness of your sins, <b>being covered, purified</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"be the means through which <b>someone else comes to repentance</b>\" → you experience the forgiveness you seek</li><li class=\"d0\">Want <b>peace</b> in your life? \"go and be a <b>peacemaker</b>\" - true across the board</li><li class=\"d0\">Particular case of <b>Hebrews</b>: faith (= faithfulness) is the <b>substance of things hoped for</b></li><li class=\"d1\">things God promised, that we want to receive, <b>become real to us when we're faithful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">faithful to Christ, doing what he commanded</li></ul>"},{"t":2476.12,"lv":2,"h":"End of James; looking to 1 Peter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"That's where <b>St. James's epistle ends</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Next: <b>1 Peter</b> - introduction to the <b>epistles of Peter</b>, and <b>Jude</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first time through the Bible he'll go <b>out of order</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>2 Peter and Jude</b> have a \"very interesting\" relationship, though <b>1-3 John</b> sit between them in our New Testament</li><li class=\"d1\">relationship of the two was a question on his <b>doctoral comprehensive exams</b> - \"going to inflict it on all of you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>John</b> (epistles), and what's left in the New Testament is <b>Revelation</b></li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week, Lord willing: <b>1 Peter</b> - intro to the epistles of <b>Peter and Jude</b>; going <b>out of order</b> (2 Peter and Jude together), then <b>John</b>, then <b>Revelation</b>"});
